{"id":"u-7bc11d6c-951d-4d8b-82ad-dfebb86072f8","src":"community","title":"The High Volume Workout for Natural Bodybuilders","author":"Mithosphere","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/NcGSpM-the-high-volume-workout-plan-for-natural-bodybuild","desc":"Program by Christian Thibaudeau\n\nNatural Bodybuilders and Volume\nTonnage is important, but when it comes to hypertrophy and the natural lifter, there's an optimal dose. If natural lifters go overboard on volume, they'll burn out their nervous system or skyrocket their cortisol – both of which will stall gains. \n\nThe Two Kinds of Bodybuilders\n1. Volume People: They normally don't push each set as hard because they want to be capable of doing the planned volume without crashing. \n\n2. Intensity People: These are people who prefer to do fewer work sets but push these extra hard – to failure (or very close to it) or even beyond.\n\nThe intensity people tend to kill themselves and get worse results when they use higher volume because they can't scale down their effort. It's all-out or nothing.\n\nThe volume crowd often burns out on high-intensity programs because of the high adrenaline/cortisol they produce. \n\nNatural Volume Lifting\nYes, the higher the volume, the greater the cortisol production. But other factors are involved. It's possible that 10 sets of 8 reps at RPE 7 could cause less of a cortisol increase than 5 sets of 8 at RPE 9. Especially if, in your case, intensity causes more cortisol release than volume.\n\nExcess volume will be a problem for most natural lifters; others will need more volume to stimulate growth because they get just as much cortisol release from pushing themselves to the limit, even when they do a lower number of sets. The solution for them is to keep the RPE per set lower when using a higher volume approach.\n\nRate of Perceived Effort\nEvery week in the four-week block, you increase tonnage by adding more reps while using the same weight. Then, on the next block, you increase the weight and lower the starting reps, once again working your way up.\n\nHow Often Should I Train Using This Approach?\nFor natural lifters, training frequency is more important than it is for enhanced lifters. For natties, the workout is responsible for 80-90% of the increase in protein synthesis that will lead to muscle growth. Three times a week is what natural lifters should shoot for, and twice a week is better than once.\n\nVolume Requires Workout Nutrition\nRemember, the three main reasons cortisol is released during a workout are:\n1. The need for energy mobilization\n2. Getting close to your limit on a set (or several sets)\n3. Using loads that create psychological stress or a \"fear\" response\n\nWith this volume approach, you rarely, if ever, reach a point where factors two and three come into play. So the main driver of cortisol will be the need to mobilize stored energy to fuel your efforts and maintain a stable blood sugar level.","level":["Novice","Intermediate","Advanced"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":60,"joins":0,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-09-14T22:35:22.733Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Whole Body A","Whole Body B","Whole Body C","Abs/Loaded Carries"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]},{"n":"Romanian Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Bench Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]},{"n":"Single Arm Row (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Seated Shoulder Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]},{"n":"Pull-Up (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]}]}]},{"ex":[{"ss":[{"n":"Leg Extension","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]},{"n":"Lying Leg Curl","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Pec Deck (Machine)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]},{"n":"Chest Supported Row (Machine)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Skull Crusher (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":7,"x":4}]},{"n":"B